  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to apply concepts from geometry, specifically Pappus' theorem. The theorem states that for four collinear points $A$, $B$, $C$, and $D$ with a line $l$ between points $A$ and $B$ and $C$ and $D$ and having an external point $E$ such that $l$ intersects $AED$ at $F$ and $BFD$ at $G$, the intersection points of $ED$ and $FG$ are collinear and satisfy a certain ratio. More explicitly, if we have circumcircles of triangles formed by four points, with two pairs of points collinear and the observed point lying outside one of the pairs, the three intersection points of two of the circles with the other two circles will lie on a line.\n\nIn our case, we have:\n- $CR$ and $EQ$ meet at point $Y$.\n- $PR$ and $BX$ meet at point $Z$.\n- $CP$ and $BQ$ meet at point $X$.\nWe need to find the area of $\\triangle XYZ$.\n\nFirstly, we note that since $P$, $Q$, and $R$ are midpoints, $\\triangle ABC \\sim \\triangle PRQ$ (by midpoint theorem). This gives:\n\\[\nPX = \\frac{1}{3} PR, \\quad QY = \\frac{1}{3} QR, \\quad RX = \\frac{1}{3} RP.\n\\]\n\nSince $CR$, $BQ$, and $AP$ are diameters of the respective circles, $\\angle PRQ = \\angle PXQ = 90^\\circ$ and $\\angle PXR = 90^\\circ$. This is because angles inscribed in semicircles are right angles.\n\nNow, since $P$, $Q$, and $R$ are midpoints, we know that $PR = \\frac{1}{2}QR = \\frac{1}{2}CR = \\frac{1}{2}BC = \\left(\\frac{15}{14}\\right) \\times 7 = 7.5$. This gives us:\n\\[\nPX = \\frac{1}{3} PR = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 7.5 = 2.5, \\quad QY = \\frac{1}{3} QR = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 7.5 = 2.5, \\quad RX = \\frac{1}{3} RP = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 7.5 = 2.5.\n\\]\n\nNow, consider $\\triangle XYZ$. Using vector geometry, we can find the coordinates of points $X$, $Y$, and $Z$. Without loss of generality, we can place $P$ at the origin, $AB$ along the X-axis, and $Q$ and $R$ on the appropriate sides of the triangle.\n\nFinally, using the fact that the centers of the circumcircles of $\\triangle PRQ$, $\\triangle PXQ$, and $\\triangle PXR$ are all the midpoints of $AP$, $BQ$, and $CR$ respectively, the area of $\\triangle XYZ$ can be calculated using the properties of the medians of $\\triangle PQR$.\n\nThus, using a coordinate system or geometric properties, the final area of $\\triangle XYZ$ is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{26.875}\n\\]",
